SCIENCE GRAMMAR, MAJORITY-CASE 
Finite-dimensional classical LTI systems, finite-dimensional closed quantum systems,
projective measurement, and ordinary classical post-processing.
All continuum fields, general Lindblad dynamics, and infinite-dimensional spaces excluded.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
0. TYPES AND BASIC OBJECTS
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Scalars:        a, b, c ∈ ℝ or ℂ
Vectors:        x, u, y, E, B, ψ ∈ ℝⁿ or ℂⁿ
Matrices:       A, B, C, D, G, H, U ∈ ℂ^{n×n}
Operators:      linear maps (classical) or unitaries (quantum)
States:
  Classical:    x(t) ∈ ℝⁿ
  Quantum:      |ψ⟩ ∈ ℋ, ρ ∈ ℒ(ℋ)
Composition:    (G₂∘G₁)x = G₂(G₁x)
Tensor:         (A ⊗ B)(x ⊗ y) = (Ax) ⊗ (By)
Measurement:    projectors Π_i = |i⟩⟨i|
Feedback:       fixed-point equation y = G(u ± H y)
Substitution:   replace subdiagram D by equivalent operator G_D

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1. SHARED STRUCTURAL OPERATORS
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
1.1 Series (cascade)
    y = G₂(G₁x) ⇒ y = (G₂∘G₁)x
    Order matters when operators do not commute.

1.2 Parallel
    Classical: y = (G₁ + G₂)x
    Quantum (non-interacting subsystems only): U_total = U₁ ⊗ U₂
    Addition ≠ tensor; domains are distinct.

1.3 Feedback (fixed-point)
    y = G(u ± H y)
    ⇒ (I ∓ G H) y = G u
    ⇒ y = (I ∓ G H)⁻¹ G u   only when inverse exists.
    Scalar commuting special case:
      y = (G / (1 ∓ G H)) u

1.4 Takeoff / copy
    Classical: free duplication y₁ = x, y₂ = x
    Quantum:  no-cloning; only isometries or explicit measurement.

1.5 Substitution
    D ⇒ G_D once input-output map is known.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2. CLASSICAL DYNAMICS (FINITE-DIMENSIONAL LTI)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
2.1 State-space equations
    ẋ = A x + B u
    y  = C x + D u

2.2 Transfer function (Laplace)
    G(s) = C (sI − A)⁻¹ B + D
    y(s) = G(s) u(s)

2.3 Closed-loop with controller K
    u = −K y
    y = C x
    Closed-loop A matrix:
      A_cl = A − B (I + D K)⁻¹ K C
    Scalar G/(1+GH) is the special commuting case.

2.4 Stability (continuous time)
    System stable ⇔ Re(λ_i(A)) < 0 for all eigenvalues λ_i.

2.5 Final-value theorem (requires pole condition)
    lim_{t→∞} y(t) = lim_{s→0} s Y(s)
    Valid only if all poles of sY(s) lie in Re(s) < 0 except possibly at s=0.

2.6 Linearization
    f(x) ≈ f(x₀) + J(x₀)(x − x₀)

2.7 Modal decomposition
    A = V Λ V⁻¹ ⇒ decoupled modes.

2.8 Superposition
    y = Σ Gᵢ uᵢ  (linearity)

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
3. ELECTROMAGNETISM (DISCRETE, FINITE-DIMENSIONAL FORM)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
3.1 Maxwell’s equations (vacuum, differential form)
    ∇·E = ρ/ε₀
    ∇·B = 0
    ∇×E = − ∂B/∂t
    ∇×B = μ₀ J + μ₀ ε₀ ∂E/∂t

3.2 Discrete finite-dimensional approximation
    Replace ∇ by matrices D_div, D_curl on a chosen grid:
      D_div E⃗ = ρ⃗ / ε₀
      D_div B⃗ = 0
      D_curl E⃗ = − dB⃗/dt
      D_curl B⃗ = μ₀ J⃗ + μ₀ ε₀ dE⃗/dt
    Resulting system is an LTI or descriptor system and falls under classical rules.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
4. THERMODYNAMICS AND STATISTICAL MECHANICS
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
4.1 First law (closed system)
    dU = δQ − δW
    Constitutive/balance relation; becomes a block only after choosing ports.

4.2 Ideal gas
    p V = N k_B T
    Constitutive relation; not a dynamical block until variables are chosen.

4.3 Boltzmann distribution (finite discrete states)
    P_i = exp(−E_i / (k_B T)) / Z
    Z = Σ_i exp(−E_i / (k_B T))

4.4 Entropy
    Classical discrete:
      S = −k_B Σ_i P_i ln P_i
    Information-theoretic:
      H(X) = − Σ_i p_i log₂ p_i

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
5. QUANTUM MECHANICS (FINITE-DIMENSIONAL, CLOSED SYSTEMS)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
5.1 Schrödinger equation
    i ℏ d|ψ⟩/dt = H |ψ⟩
    Solution (time-independent H):
      |ψ(t)⟩ = e^{−i H t / ℏ} |ψ(0)⟩
      U(t) = e^{−i H t / ℏ} (unitary)

5.2 Von Neumann equation (density operator)
    ρ̇ = −(i/ℏ)[H, ρ]
    ρ(t) = U(t) ρ(0) U(t)†

5.3 Projective measurement
    Π_i = |i⟩⟨i|
    Probabilities:
      p_i = ⟨ψ| Π_i |ψ⟩ = |⟨i|ψ⟩|²
    Post-measurement state:
      |ψ'⟩ = Π_i |ψ⟩ / √p_i
    Only the classical record i crosses the hybrid boundary.

5.4 Partial trace (discarding subsystem)
    ρ_A = Tr_B(ρ_AB)
    Completely positive, trace-preserving; not unitary.

5.5 Tensor product for non-interacting subsystems
    If H_total = H_A ⊗ I + I ⊗ H_B:
      U_total(t) = U_A(t) ⊗ U_B(t)

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
6. QUANTUM INFORMATION AND ALGORITHMS
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
6.1 Pauli matrices
    X = |0⟩⟨1| + |1⟩⟨0|
    Z = |0⟩⟨0| − |1⟩⟨1|
    Commutation: σᵢ σⱼ = ± σⱼ σᵢ

6.2 Hadamard
    H|0⟩ = (|0⟩ + |1⟩)/√2
    H|1⟩ = (|0⟩ − |1⟩)/√2
    H² = I

6.3 Controlled-unitary
    CU: |c⟩|ψ⟩ ↦ |c⟩ U^c |ψ⟩
    Circuit identities allow commuting under known conditions.

6.4 Quantum Fourier Transform (QFT)
    QFT |k⟩ = (1/√N) Σ_{j=0}^{N−1} e^{2πi k j / N} |j⟩
    QFT⁻¹ QFT = I

6.5 Period finding (Shor)
    Measured phase ≈ k/r
    Continued-fraction expansion yields candidate r
    Single sample does not guarantee exact order.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
7. INFORMATION THEORY AND CHANNELS
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
7.1 Bayes’ rule
    P(A|B) = P(B|A) P(A) / P(B)

7.2 Mutual information
    I(X;Y) = Σ_{x,y} p(x,y) log₂ [ p(x,y) / (p(x)p(y)) ]

7.3 Classical channel (finite-dimensional)
    p_out = T p_in
    T stochastic: T_{ij} ≥ 0, Σ_j T_{ij} = 1

7.4 Quantum channel (CPTP)
    ρ_out = Σ_k E_k ρ_in E_k†
    Σ_k E_k† E_k = I

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
8. BLOCK-DIAGRAM LINKAGE (CLASSICAL + QUANTUM + HYBRID)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
8.1 Classical blocks
    G(s) = C (sI − A)⁻¹ B + D
    Composition associative: G₂∘G₁
    Feedback is fixed-point reduction when inverse exists.

8.2 Quantum blocks
    Unitary evolution: |ψ⟩ ↦ U |ψ⟩
    Measurement: |ψ⟩ ↦ {p_i, |ψ_i⟩}
    Only classical record crosses boundary.

8.3 Hybrid boundary
    Quantum evolution → projective measurement → classical arithmetic
    Classical post-processing: gcd, continued fractions, logic, control.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
9. SCOPE AND LIMITATIONS
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
• Finite-dimensional classical LTI systems only.
• Finite-dimensional closed quantum systems only.
• Projective measurement only.
• Classical post-processing only.
• Discrete Maxwell only after grid discretization.
• No continuum PDEs, no Lindblad, no infinite-dimensional Hilbert spaces.

Within this scope, every equation above is a valid block,
and the thirty diagram rules apply exactly:
  – Composition associative
  – Feedback = fixed-point reduction (when inverse exists)
  – Measurement = sole classical–quantum boundary
  – Substitution = replace block by equivalent operator
